Package: remotedesktop-client
Version: @VERSION@
Section: net
Priority: optional
Architecture: @ARCHITECTURE@
Maintainer: Zeqian GAO <zeqian_gao@outlook.com>
Homepage: https://github.com/gaozeqian/RemoteDesktop
Depends: ca-certificates, debconf (>= 0.5) | debconf-2.0, libavcodec62, libavformat62, libavutil60, libc6, libei1, libfontconfig1, libfreetype6, libgcc-s1, libgl1, libicu78 | libicu77 | libicu76 | libicu74 | libicu72, libpipewire-0.3-0t64 | libpipewire-0.3-0, libpulse0, libssl3t64 | libssl3, libstdc++6, libswscale9, libx11-6, libxcb1, libxcursor1, libxext6, libxfixes3, libxi6, libxrandr2, libxrender1, zlib1g
Recommends: xdg-desktop-portal-gnome | xdg-desktop-portal-kde | xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
Description: Remote Desktop client (controls another computer)
 Shows another computer's screen in a window and sends this machine's keyboard
 and pointer to it.
 .
 The installation asks for the signaling server's address and, when that
 address is wss:// and the server uses a self-signed certificate, for the
 certificate to trust. No certificate is needed for a ws:// address or for one
 signed by a public authority.
 .
 The window is the whole of it: this package installs no service, and installing
 it does not register this machine as a device. While the window runs, though,
 it can also act as the controlled end when the separate remotedesktop-host
 package is not installed (and its service is not running) — the local
 two-instance workflow uses exactly that. The host package is what makes a
 machine reachable while nobody is sitting at it.
 .
 The libraries above are loaded by name when the window is first drawn rather
 than at startup, so without them the program would start and then fail to show
 anything. They are the toolkit's (X11 and OpenGL for the window, PulseAudio for
 sound, PipeWire and libei for the in-process controlled end) and the video
 codec's (libavcodec for decoding, libavformat for recording). dpkg-shlibdeps
 cannot see any of them.
